<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Mustarshid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1092">1092</a>–<a href="/years/1135">1135</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Mustarshid was the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> from 1118 to 1135 who tried to free the caliphate from Seljuk control by raising his own army. He was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Mas'ud near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> in 1135 and taken prisoner. Soon afterwards he was murdered, reportedly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-assassins">Assassins</a>, though the caliphs later regained control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-mustarshid.jpg" alt="Al-Mustarshid" width="192" height="291" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Mustarshid_Billah_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu Mansur al-Fadl ibn Ahmad al-Mustazhir was born in 1092, the son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> al-Mustazhir and a Slavic concubine named Lubanah. He took the regnal name al-Mustarshid Billah and succeeded his father in 1118. His only wife was Amira Khatun, a daughter of the Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-sanjar">Ahmad Sanjar</a>, whom he married in 1124.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Al-Mustarshid gained more independence while the Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-ii-seljuk-sultan">Mahmud II</a> was engaged in the east. In 1122 he deposed and imprisoned his vizier Amid al-dawla Jalal al-Din <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-ibn-ali">Hasan ibn Ali</a>, and Mahmud then imposed Ahmad ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a> as vizier. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> also installed Aqsunqur al-Bursuqi as commander-in-chief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. The Mazyadid chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubays-ibn-sadaqa">Dubays ibn Sadaqa</a> fought Seljuk forces in Iraq, and the Seljuk commander Zengi defeated him at the Battle of Mubarraqiyya in March 1123.</p><p>In 1125 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> himself rebelled. He sent an army to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wasit">Wasit</a> but was defeated near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and confined to his palace the following year. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> pillaged his palace, and he had to pay a large ransom to make peace. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-ii-seljuk-sultan">Mahmud II</a> died, the Seljuk west fell into civil war. On 20 June 1132 Zengi and Dubays, invited by Sanjar, met the caliph's forces at Hisn al-Baramika, north of Baghdad. Al-Mustarshid prevailed despite their superior numbers, then pursued Zengi to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> and besieged him there for three months without success. The campaign was a milestone in reviving the military power of the caliphate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Not long after Zengi's siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1134, al-Mustarshid marched against the Seljuk sultan Mas'ud, who had received the title in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in January 1133 from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> himself. The armies met near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>, where the caliph's troops deserted him and he was taken prisoner. He was pardoned on promising not to leave his palace, but was found murdered in the caliphal tent while reading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>. He died on 29 August 1135. The murder is supposed to have been the work of an emissary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-assassins">Assassins</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismaili-state">Nizari Ismailis</a> listed him among their victims. Modern historians have suspected that Mas'ud instigated it, although the chroniclers Ibn al-Athir and Ibn al-Jawzi did not speculate on the matter.</p><p>His son al-Rashid Billah succeeded him and was deposed on 17 August 1136, after the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> rose against him. Al-Rashid's half-brother al-Muqtafi followed him on 17 September 1136.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Mustarshid” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sultan Mas'ud defeated the caliph <b>al-Mustarshid</b>, who was murdered in 1135, but the Abbasid caliphs gradually regained control of Iraq, and Muhammad II's siege of Baghdad in 1157 failed.</blockquote><a href="/state/seljuk-sultanate-of-iraq">Seljuk Sultanate of Iraq</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=1119" aria-label="Baghdad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5684 954 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Al-Mustarshid"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6134 1224h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6134 1224h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=1119">What was here in 1119? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
